\section{Make a Root a Machine Location}

A precise collector knows exactly which machine locations
contain references at a point where the program can stop. A
root census includes interpreter frames, compiled frames,
globals, and registered native handles. A slot containing a
reference has two roles in a moving collector: it preserves
reachability and it receives a replacement address. A census
that collects pointer values in an ordinary array can preserve
the first role while failing the second. Walk the original
locations or define how each collected value is copied back
before execution resumes.

The map for a compiled function is generally not a bitmap of
source variables. Register allocation may assign the same stack
word to a pointer in one part of the function and an integer
later. A precise map identifies final machine locations, so it
must reflect register allocation, spill insertion, frame layout,
and the convention for identifying a stopped frame's program
counter. For a caller suspended in a callee, the saved return
address is a useful map key \cite{appel2004modern}. For the
youngest frame stopped at a poll, the runtime and compiler need
an equally explicit convention, such as a poll-specific resume
PC paired with a saved register context.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
At a call & Meaning & Collector action \\
\hline
Frame slot 24 & Live base reference & Visit and update \\
Saved register \(r_4\) & Derived address & Rebuild from updated base \\
Frame slot 40 & Dead old reference & Do not visit \\
Saved register \(r_7\) & Integer subtotal & Do not visit \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\Cref{fig:visual-411} distinguishes selecting the caller's map
from finding its physical register storage. A callee's spill
location can hold a caller's callee-saved root. The caller's
map describes its logical register; unwind information tells
the walker where the value now resides. The collector must
update the storage from which the caller will actually resume.

