% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Repair Derived Addresses After Visiting Bases}

\begin{verbatim}
enumerate_thread(thread):
    for frame in unwind(thread.saved_context):
        map := lookup_map(frame.code, frame.stop_key)
        for entry in map.base_roots:
            record_root(resolve(frame, entry))
        for spec in map.derived_roots:
            base := resolve(frame, spec.base)
            derived := resolve(frame, spec.derived)
            delta := known_or_computed_delta(spec, base, derived)
            record_derived(base, derived, delta)

repair_roots():
    for slot in recorded_base_roots:
        slot.store(relocate_or_keep(slot.load()))
    for record in recorded_derived_roots:
        record.derived.store(record.base.load() + record.delta)
\end{verbatim}

The example assumes a non-null base and valid offset arithmetic,
and a root list with duplicate storage locations normalized.
Capture derived offsets before any update. If a vector base
moves from address 1000 to 4000 and a cursor was 1032, the
cursor must become 4032. Looking up 1032 as if it were an object
start is incorrect. Retain the base in machine-level liveness
even if the cursor is its only future use. An alternative is to
forbid derived pointers across safepoints and recompute them
after each pause. That may reduce metadata and simplify the
collector, but increases recomputation and register pressure.
Decide before optimizing address calculations across calls.

The maps and unwind data have lifetimes too. Invalidating a JIT
entry point prevents new calls; it does not eliminate suspended
frames in the old code. Keep its metadata until all such frames,
and any suspended continuations referencing that code, have
drained. A code cache that recycles the same virtual addresses
needs a version or equivalent synchronization, so a stale PC
cannot accidentally select the replacement function's map.

\section{Define Where a Thread Can Stop}

In a cooperative design, allocation slow paths, calls that may
allocate, and loop-backedge polls provide known stop states.
Without polls a long nonallocating loop can indefinitely delay
a collection requested by another thread. A poll must not
advertise a stable context until compiler and runtime agree on
which registers and frame state are visible. Arbitrarily
suspending a thread in the middle of an untracked object store
does not establish that contract. Measure time to reach the
safepoint separately from time spent performing collection; a
large handshake delay is not fixed by speeding up marking.

Native transitions need their own rule. A blocking native call
can be treated as outside managed execution only after publishing
its handles and relinquishing access to raw movable storage. A
pin is an alternative when the collector supports pinning, but
the pin must have both a lifetime and a rooting policy. A
registered handle that survives one callback is not automatically
valid after the owning scope closes. If the native code reenters
managed execution, it participates as a mutator again and
reloads any movable references through their handles.
This is the collector-side contract behind the bounded
pinning scopes and callback contexts in
\Cref{ch:ext-389,ch:ext-390,ch:ext-391}.

\section{Test Locations After the Backend Changes Them}

Construct a compiled function with a live pointer across one
call and a scalar across a second call using the same spill
slot. Force relocation inside both callees. The first must
rewrite the slot, and the second must leave its integer intact.
Run another variant in which the only visible use after the
call is a derived address. Compare slot and register locations
against a compiler debug dump at each native stop key, and
exercise a callee-save register spilled more than one frame
deep. Also force collection during interpreter-to-JIT transitions
and after JIT invalidation. A missing metadata entry should be
an explicit diagnostic at the boundary, not a request to fall
back to guessing which machine words are pointers.

\section{Keep Safepoints Separate From Reconstruction State}

An optimizing compiler can maintain two records at the same
machine address. A deoptimization record says how to reconstruct
an interpreter state, perhaps materializing a scalar-replaced
pair. A stack map says which locations already contain references
and need updating for collection. Suppose the pair does not
exist in the heap but its two pointer fields live in registers.
The stack map lists those registers, not a fictional root for
the pair. If later deoptimization constructs the pair, it uses
the updated values. A compact shared format may encode both
records, but a consumer must still know which parts it needs.

Control flow can make a single return address ambiguous if the
compiler merges a slow path from two source points. Callers
can share one machine map only if they present compatible
root locations to the shared call, for example by performing
edge moves into canonical spill slots. Filling a union of the
locations with whatever bytes happen to be there is not a
precise map. A tagged-value interpreter can test a slot tag,
but an untagged compiled slot might contain an ordinary integer
in one predecessor and a pointer in the other.

Exception transfer changes the applicable state. During
unwinding the runtime should use a known safe state or prevent
collection during any interval without one. After landing at a
handler, it cannot keep using the map of the abandoned call.
A saved exception object may itself be a runtime root before
the handler has established a managed frame. Test an exception
raised through compiled and native frames with a collection
at the permitted transition, not just on normal return.

You can validate maps with an independent interpreter of the
compiler's location assignments. At each stop key, compare the
emitted entries against reconstructible live reference values
and inspect packed tables for signed-offset or register-number
encoding mistakes. Such comparisons catch structural errors;
run with simultaneous aliases and move the referenced objects
to check that the unwinder and entry stubs interpret the
metadata in the same way as the backend that wrote it.
